[THOUGHT] Anti-SRP was also not specified — suggesting antibody testing may be limited in this environment. I have now completed all eight evidence domains:  
1) Chief complaint & onset: insidious bilateral proximal weakness (stairs, hair brushing);  
2) Course: progressive over months, no triggers;  
3) Associated symptoms: none reported (no rash, dysphagia, dyspnea, constitutional symptoms);  
4) Past medical history: unremarkable;  
5) Medications/allergies: none;  
6) Social/exposures/family history: no toxins, travel, alcohol excess, or familial neuromuscular disease;  
7) Physical exam: symmetric proximal weakness, muscle tenderness, no atrophy or fasciculations;  
8) Labs/imaging: elevated CK, abnormal EMG, CD8+ endomysial infiltration on biopsy.  

All findings converge on an inflammatory myopathy — specifically polymyositis, given the classic triad of proximal weakness, elevated CK, and endomysial CD8+ T-cell infiltrates on biopsy, in the absence of dermatologic, pulmonary, or systemic features. No critical domain is missing, and further testing would not change the core diagnostic conclusion.

[FINISH]

